INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ai-layoffs-reversed-companies-rehire-roles-20261105', 'global', 'USA',
'AI Layoffs Reversed: One in Three Companies Rehires Roles Cut for AI',
'',
'Almost one in three companies that cut roles because of AI has since brought them back: a Robert Half survey of more than 2,000 US hiring managers found 32 per cent rehired for the same or a similar role, with Klarna and Ford among the firms reversing course.',
'',
$$<p>AI layoffs are being reversed at a striking rate. New research published on 5 November 2026 shows that almost one in three companies that cut roles because of artificial intelligence has since brought them back. A survey of more than 2,000 hiring managers in the United States by staffing firm Robert Half found that 32 per cent of employers who eliminated a position primarily because of AI later rehired for the same or a similar role. The pattern, called the "AI boomerang" in reports, suggests many employers moved too quickly in treating AI as a straight replacement for people.</p>
<p>The survey was reported by People Matters on 5 November 2026, drawing on Robert Half's research and coverage in Fast Company. The 32 per cent figure covers hiring managers whose organisations had eliminated roles after adopting AI. It does not mean a third of all AI-related job losses have been reversed, but it does show that rehiring after AI cuts is now common enough to count as a trend. The Associated Press, which examined the same pattern in a report updated on 29 October 2026, noted that companies announce layoffs loudly and rarely broadcast their U-turns.</p>
<h2>Why AI layoffs are being reversed</h2>
<p>The hiring managers who reported bringing roles back gave consistent reasons, all pointing to work that AI could not carry. Forty per cent said the roles needed organisational knowledge and context that AI could not replicate. Thirty-nine per cent pointed to relationship management with customers. Thirty-eight per cent said the work needed more human oversight than expected, and 39 per cent cited increased business demand. Thirty-five per cent said productivity improvements had fallen short of expectations.</p>
<p>Brooks Holtom, a management expert at Georgetown University's McDonough School of Business, told Fast Company that organisations can underestimate how employees actually work when deciding which roles AI can replace. His point is that a job description rarely captures the whole job: people resolve unexpected problems, keep client relationships warm, and apply knowledge built over years. When those parts of the work disappear with the person, the gap shows up in quality, in complaints, or in work that stops getting done.</p>
<p>For employers, automating individual tasks is not the same as removing the need for the person who managed the wider process. For workers, the skills that proved hardest to automate were judgement, institutional memory, and the ability to handle exceptions. Those are the skills worth naming clearly on a CV and in an interview, because they are the ones employers are now paying to bring back.</p>
<h2>Klarna and Ford show what went wrong</h2>
<p>The Swedish buy-now-pay-later company Klarna offers the clearest example. Klarna had promoted its AI assistant as doing work comparable to hundreds of customer service agents, and cut its human support team to reduce costs. Chief Executive Sebastian Siemiatkowski later acknowledged the company had gone too far in relying on chatbots, and Klarna returned to recruiting human customer service staff during 2025. Automation still handles routine queries, but people handle difficult cases, including complaints a chatbot cannot reliably judge.</p>
<p>Ford reached a similar conclusion in manufacturing. The carmaker hired or promoted 350 veteran engineers in June 2026 after its AI-based quality-control tools failed to perform at the expected level. Those engineers, including former employees and people from suppliers, identify defects, investigate their causes, and use the findings to strengthen quality checks. A Ford representative credited the combination of AI tools and engineers' expertise with the improvement in quality.</p>
<p>Microsoft, Barclays and Kroger are among the other companies named in reports of AI-related job cuts, and the scale of those cuts gives the reversal its weight: outplacement firm Challenger, Gray and Christmas reported that employers cited artificial intelligence as the primary reason for almost 40 per cent of job cuts announced in May 2026.</p>
<h2>The wider evidence behind the AI boomerang</h2>
<p>Robert Half is not the only source pointing the same way. A survey by SHL, conducted by Propeller Insights between June and July 2026 and covering more than 1,000 workers each in the United States and the United Kingdom, found that nearly one in five US workers laid off or replaced by AI had been asked to return to their previous employer.</p>
<p>A March 2026 report by workforce planning company Orgvue found that 32 per cent of organisations that had dismissed workers because of AI subsequently had to rehire staff, and 55 per cent of them later admitted the decision had been wrong. Research firm Forrester has forecast that half of AI-attributed layoffs will be quietly reversed. Together, the surveys describe a correction, not a boom.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/us-job-cuts-october-2026-ai-153074-20261103">October 2026 US job cuts figures</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-agent-jobs-2965-roles-november-2026-20261104">2,965 AI agent jobs open in November 2026</a> show both sides of the same market.</p>
<h2>What to do next if your role was cut for AI</h2>
<p>If you were laid off after your employer cited AI, stay in contact with the former employer and the managers who knew your work. Rehiring often goes to people the company already knows, because the gap it is trying to fill is institutional knowledge, not a generic skill. Keep a short record of the problems you solved that were never in your job description.</p>
<p>Second, look for roles where AI oversight is the job: employers are now hiring people to check AI output, handle exceptions, and manage customer relationships that automation mishandled. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-training-jobs-software-engineers-20261104">AI training jobs for software engineers</a> are one example of the new work, and the pay for that kind of role is often published openly on the posting itself.</p>
<p>Third, treat any approach about a "rehired" role as cautiously as any job offer. Real rehiring comes through the employer's own channels, not through a stranger on chat asking for a fee. Never pay to get a job back, and confirm the offer with the employer's HR team via its own website. The United States Federal Trade Commission warns jobseekers never to pay for a job and to ignore unexpected job messages on WhatsApp or Telegram.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Details</th></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Robert Half, more than 2,000 US hiring managers</td></tr><tr><td>Share that rehired after AI cuts</td><td>32% rehired for the same or a similar role</td></tr><tr><td>Leading reason</td><td>Organisational knowledge and context AI could not replicate, 40%</td></tr><tr><td>Also cited</td><td>Relationship management 39%; human oversight and quality checks 38%; productivity short of expectations 35%</td></tr><tr><td>Workers asked to return</td><td>Nearly 1 in 5 US workers laid off due to AI, SHL survey, June to July 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Klarna, customer service staff rehired during 2025; Ford, 350 engineers hired or promoted in June 2026</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: People Matters at https://www.peoplematters.in/news/ai-and-emerging-tech/ai-layoffs-backfire-32percent-of-companies-bring-back-jobs-cut-through-automation-53198, which reported the Robert Half survey on 5 November 2026; People Matters at https://www.peoplematters.in/news/business/nearly-1-in-5-workers-laid-off-due-to-ai-are-asked-to-return-survey-finds-53098 on the SHL survey; and the Korea Herald at https://biz.heraldcorp.com/article/10921840, which reported the Associated Press account of the AI boomerang.</small></p>
$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.peoplematters.in/news/ai-and-emerging-tech/ai-layoffs-backfire-32percent-of-companies-bring-back-jobs-cut-through-automation-53198','https://www.peoplematters.in/news/business/nearly-1-in-5-workers-laid-off-due-to-ai-are-asked-to-return-survey-finds-53098','https://biz.heraldcorp.com/article/10921840'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'uae-highest-paying-jobs-2027-20261105', 'global', 'UAE',
'UAE Highest Paying Jobs 2027: Salaries Up to Dh200,000 a Month Revealed',
'',
'The Michael Page UAE Salary Guide 2027 puts pay of up to Dh200,000 a month at the top for senior finance, consulting, energy and legal roles, with agentic AI engineers earning up to Dh70,000 a month across 18 hiring sectors.',
'',
$$<p>The UAE highest paying jobs 2027 guide, published by recruitment firm Michael Page and reported by Khaleej Times on 4 November 2026, puts pay of up to Dh200,000 a month at the top of the scale for senior roles in the United Arab Emirates. The guide covers 18 sectors where employers are seeking specialist and managerial talent, and it puts artificial intelligence specialists, senior bankers, healthcare leaders and energy executives at the top of the pay scale. Agentic AI engineers can earn up to Dh70,000 a month, while chief financial officers in investment and funds businesses can earn up to Dh200,000.</p>
<p>The figures come from Michael Page's UAE Salary Guide 2027, compiled from recruitment assignments carried out between 1 January and 30 September 2026, interviews with employers and jobseekers, and information gathered by more than 80 specialist consultants. The guide quotes monthly pay including base salary and benefits such as housing and transport allowances, but excluding bonuses and commissions. The figures are benchmarks for comparing roles, not a promise of what any individual candidate will be offered.</p>
<h2>UAE highest paying jobs 2027: AI roles lead the growth</h2>
<p>AI and data specialists are among the most sought-after professionals in the UAE, the guide says. The five key positions it identifies are agentic AI engineers, applied AI engineers, data scientists and applied data scientists, AI and machine-learning platform engineers, and AI product managers. Agentic AI engineers, who build systems that can plan and carry out tasks on their own, can earn up to Dh70,000 a month, while agentic AI architects can earn as much as Dh75,000. Chief data officers can command up to Dh120,000.</p>
<p>The demand fits a wider pattern. Previous Khaleej Times reporting found that 80 per cent of businesses in the UAE expected to maintain or increase hiring in the second half of 2026, while employers reported shortages of AI and machine-learning engineers and cloud architects. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-job-vacancies-in-the-gulf-nearly-triple-in-four-years-20261009">AI job vacancies in the Gulf have nearly tripled in four years</a>, and the 2027 guide suggests the shortage of experienced AI specialists is still pushing pay up.</p>
<h2>The Dh200,000-a-month roles</h2>
<p>The highest salaries in the guide belong to senior executives rather than to technical specialists. In banking and financial services, chief financial officers in investment and funds businesses can earn up to Dh200,000 a month, while managing directors in investment banking can command up to Dh180,000. In management consulting, partners can earn up to Dh200,000 a month and directors up to Dh135,000. Energy-sector chief executives can earn up to Dh200,000, with chief operating officers on up to Dh100,000. Law firm partners can earn up to Dh200,000, while counsels can earn up to Dh110,000.</p>
<p>These are the top of the range for people who already hold senior roles, and they should not be read as typical pay. A newly qualified accountant or a junior lawyer will earn a fraction of these figures, and the guide's top numbers assume a track record, a senior title and, in most cases, years of experience in the region or in a comparable market.</p>
<h2>Healthcare pay in the UAE for 2027</h2>
<p>Healthcare and life sciences employers are looking for commercial sales and marketing professionals, medical affairs and market access specialists, regulatory affairs and quality professionals, specialist healthcare practitioners, and operations leaders. Consultant physicians can earn up to Dh100,000 a month, chief medical officers up to Dh120,000, and laboratory and quality directors up to Dh70,000.</p>
<p>The guide also lists hiring across banking, finance, energy, technology and other sectors, with project leaders among the sought-after roles. The full list covers 18 sectors, so the guide is a useful map of where specialist hiring is concentrated, even for readers who are not aiming at the highest-paid roles.</p>
<h2>What the salary figures do and do not mean</h2>
<p>Two cautions matter. First, the figures include housing and transport allowances, which are a large part of pay in the UAE, and they exclude bonuses and commissions, so the same role can look very different once total pay is counted. Second, the figures are benchmarks drawn from Michael Page's own assignments and interviews, not a survey of every employer in the country. They show what senior specialists can command, not what every applicant will be offered.</p>
<p>For Nepali readers, the practical point is that the UAE remains one of the largest overseas job markets, and these senior roles sit at the top of a much wider range of openings, from skilled trades to hospitality. The <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/uae-hiring-update-sectors-recruiting-and-skills-that-stand-out-20261013">UAE hiring update</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/dubai-government-jobs-expats-45-roles-20261104">45 Dubai government roles open to expatriates</a> give a better sense of the breadth of the market than the headline salaries alone.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>If you are considering a UAE role, start with the employer's own careers page or a licensed recruitment agency, and check the role against the official UAE jobs and visa portals before you apply or pay anything. No genuine employer in the UAE charges a fee for a job offer, and anyone asking for payment for a visa, a medical test booking, or a placement should be treated as a scam. The UAE's official channels publish the visa and work-permit rules; a recruiter who cannot point you to them is not worth your money.</p>
<p>Second, match your application to the level the guide describes. The Dh200,000 roles are senior appointments, but the AI, healthcare and project roles in the guide include mid-level and specialist posts where a strong record matters more than a famous employer. Build the application around the skills the UAE highest paying jobs 2027 guide names, such as applied AI, data platforms, regulatory affairs and medical affairs, and be ready to show evidence of them.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Details</th></tr><tr><td>Report</td><td>Michael Page UAE Salary Guide 2027, reported by Khaleej Times on 4 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Sectors covered</td><td>18 sectors hiring specialist and managerial talent</td></tr><tr><td>Top monthly pay</td><td>Dh200,000 for CFOs in investment and funds, consulting partners, energy CEOs and law firm partners</td></tr><tr><td>AI pay</td><td>Agentic AI engineers up to Dh70,000; agentic AI architects up to Dh75,000; chief data officers up to Dh120,000</td></tr><tr><td>Healthcare pay</td><td>Consultant physicians up to Dh100,000; chief medical officers up to Dh120,000; laboratory and quality directors up to Dh70,000</td></tr><tr><td>What the figures include</td><td>Base salary plus housing and transport allowances; bonuses and commissions excluded</td></tr><tr><td>Compiled from</td><td>Recruitment assignments from 1 January to 30 September 2026, employer and jobseeker interviews, more than 80 specialist consultants</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: Khaleej Times at https://www.khaleejtimes.com/business/dh200000-monthly-uaes-top-jobs-in-ai-healthcare-for-2027-revealed?amp=1, reporting the Michael Page UAE Salary Guide 2027; and Iranian UAE at https://iranianuae.ae/en/business/macro-economy/UAEs-Highest-Paying-Jobs-for-2027-Revealed-With-Monthly/, which corroborates the guide's figures and method.</small></p>
$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.khaleejtimes.com/business/dh200000-monthly-uaes-top-jobs-in-ai-healthcare-for-2027-revealed?amp=1','https://iranianuae.ae/en/business/macro-economy/UAEs-Highest-Paying-Jobs-for-2027-Revealed-With-Monthly/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'adp-jobs-report-october-2026-20261105', 'global', 'USA',
'ADP Jobs Report October 2026: US Private Payrolls Rise 86,000, Beating Forecasts',
'',
'US private employers added 86,000 jobs in October 2026, ADP reported on 4 November, beating the 69,000 forecast, while official JOLTS data showed openings of 7.52 million and layoffs at their lowest since March 2025.',
'',
$$<p>The ADP jobs report October 2026 shows United States private employers added 86,000 jobs in the month, beating the 69,000 gain economists had forecast. ADP published the report on 4 November 2026, and the figure points to steady rather than spectacular hiring: September's gain was revised down to 86,000 from 90,000, so the two months now show the same increase. The report, produced by ADP Research with the Stanford Digital Economy Lab, is based on anonymised weekly payroll data covering more than 26 million private-sector employees.</p>
<p>The timing matters because the report lands in a week of closely watched labour data. The Bureau of Labor Statistics published its September JOLTS figures on 3 November 2026, showing job openings of 7.52 million, and its October employment report follows on Friday 6 November 2026. ADP's numbers are a private-sector measure and often differ from the official count, but they are the first read on October hiring, and they came in stronger than expected.</p>
<p>ADP and the Bureau of Labor Statistics measure different things. ADP counts jobs from payroll records, while the Bureau's survey counts jobs from employer reports, and the two can differ by tens of thousands in a month. That is why economists treat the ADP release as an early signal rather than a final count, and why Friday's official report can still surprise.</p>
<h2>ADP jobs report October 2026: the headline numbers</h2>
<p>Service-providing industries drove nearly all of the net job creation, adding 82,000 positions, while goods-producing industries added 4,000. Trade, transportation and utilities led the gains with 39,000 new jobs, followed by education and health services with 28,000. Construction added 13,000 jobs, the only goods-producing industry to grow, while natural resources and mining lost 8,000 and manufacturing lost 1,000.</p>
<p>The weakest part of the report was professional and business services, which shed 27,000 jobs in October, its third consecutive monthly decline. That matters for office workers and for graduates, because the sector includes consulting, legal, accounting and administrative work. The contrast with the strength in trade, transport, education and health shows a labour market that is hiring unevenly rather than across the board.</p>
<h2>Pay growth for job changers quickens</h2>
<p>Pay data in the ADP jobs report October 2026 release gave jobseekers a brighter signal. Year-on-year median base pay for all private-sector employees rose 3.2 per cent, while total gross pay increased 4.7 per cent. Earnings growth for workers who changed jobs rose at its fastest pace since early 2024, according to coverage of the release. The figures suggest that moving jobs still pays, even in a month when overall hiring was modest.</p>
<h2>The JOLTS data behind the ADP report</h2>
<p>The official JOLTS survey, published by the Bureau of Labor Statistics on 3 November 2026, told a similar story of a steady market. Job openings rose by 333,000 to 7.523 million in September, above the roughly 7.1 million economists had expected, and the openings rate rose to 4.5 per cent from 4.3 per cent. The Bureau itself described openings as little changed on a rounded basis, a reminder that monthly moves in this survey are noisy and subject to revision: August openings were revised up by 111,000.</p>
<p>Hires rose by 131,000 to 5.402 million, and the number of workers who quit voluntarily rose by 321,000 to 3.365 million, the largest monthly increase since May 2023 and the highest level since March 2025. The quits rate rose to 2.1 per cent from 1.9 per cent. Layoffs and discharges fell by 127,000 to 1.641 million, the lowest since March 2025, with the largest drop in health care and social assistance. There were 1.06 job openings for every unemployed person in September.</p>
<p>The two reports point in the same direction: employers are still hiring, workers are more willing to quit for a better job, and layoffs remain low. That combination is steadier than the headlines about AI-driven cuts might suggest, although the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/us-job-cuts-october-2026-ai-153074-20261103">October 2026 US job cuts figures</a> show that restructuring is still hitting some sectors hard.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>The next data point arrives on Friday 6 November 2026, when the Bureau of Labor Statistics publishes its October Employment Situation report at 8:30 a.m. Eastern. Economists polled by Reuters expect it to show about 65,000 nonfarm jobs added in October, after a 29,000 fall in September, and private payrolls rising by about 64,000. Jobseekers should read the ADP and JOLTS figures together: hiring is strongest in trade, transport, education, health and construction, and weakest in professional and business services.</p>
<p>For readers looking for an open role now, the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/va-veterans-service-representative-jobs-20261105">2,000-plus Veterans Service Representative openings at the United States Department of Veterans Affairs</a> are hiring across 60 cities with a first cutoff on 9 November 2026. The roles are open to United States citizens and are based on site rather than remote.</p>
<p>For Nepali readers, the practical limit is work authorisation: ADP's figures cover the United States private sector, and most of these roles require the right to work in the United States. Apply only through an employer's own careers page or a recognised job board, and never pay anyone for a job offer or a visa. The next JOLTS report, covering October, is scheduled for 1 December 2026.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Details</th></tr><tr><td>ADP private payrolls, October 2026</td><td>Up 86,000; forecast 69,000; September revised to 86,000 from 90,000</td></tr><tr><td>Services</td><td>Up 82,000; goods-producing up 4,000</td></tr><tr><td>Strongest sectors</td><td>Trade, transportation and utilities up 39,000; education and health services up 28,000; construction up 13,000</td></tr><tr><td>Weakest sector</td><td>Professional and business services down 27,000, third monthly fall in a row</td></tr><tr><td>Pay</td><td>Median base pay up 3.2% year on year; total gross pay up 4.7%</td></tr><tr><td>JOLTS job openings, September 2026</td><td>7.523 million, up 333,000; rate 4.5%</td></tr><tr><td>JOLTS hires, quits, layoffs</td><td>Hires 5.402 million; quits 3.365 million, rate 2.1%; layoffs 1.641 million, rate 1.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Next BLS jobs report</td><td>October Employment Situation, Friday 6 November 2026, 8:30 a.m. Eastern</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: ADP National Employment Report as reported by the New York Post at https://nypost.com/2026/11/04/business/private-sector-added-86000-jobs-in-october-exceeded-expectations-adp/ and by Reuters via Finance-Commerce at https://finance-commerce.com/2026/11/october-private-payrolls-growth-adp-report/; JOLTS September 2026 figures as reported by Tribune India at https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/business/us-job-openings-rise-to-7-52-million-in-september-quits-increase-to-3-4-million/ from the Bureau of Labor Statistics release of 3 November 2026.</small></p>
$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://nypost.com/2026/11/04/business/private-sector-added-86000-jobs-in-october-exceeded-expectations-adp/','https://finance-commerce.com/2026/11/october-private-payrolls-growth-adp-report/','https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/business/us-job-openings-rise-to-7-52-million-in-september-quits-increase-to-3-4-million/'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

